WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.309
While Waldorf Education offers a deeply creative and holistic approach,

1
00:00:04.849 --> 00:00:07.489
implementing it within a public charter school can

2
00:00:07.489 --> 00:00:10.090
present a few unique challenges for teachers.

3
00:00:10.869 --> 00:00:16.139
Waldorf Education often defers formal reading instruction until around age 7.

4
00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.989
This delay in early literacy can create noticeable pacing

5
00:00:19.989 --> 00:00:23.090
gaps when compared against state grade level standards.

6
00:00:23.709 --> 00:00:27.020
Because daily learning relies heavily on oral storytelling,

7
00:00:27.229 --> 00:00:27.649
movement,

8
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:31.229
and artistic expression rather than on standard worksheets,

9
00:00:31.569 --> 00:00:35.090
parents often find it difficult to capture written work samples.

10
00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:40.389
Waldorf families often seek to purchase specialized hands-on materials,

11
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.549
such as play silks,

12
00:00:41.659 --> 00:00:42.500
wooden items,

13
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.419
and specific crafting supplies,

14
00:00:44.709 --> 00:00:49.090
which can create confusion about what qualifies for state instructional funding.

15
00:00:49.709 --> 00:00:50.229
Finally,

16
00:00:50.389 --> 00:00:52.830
the strong Waldorf philosophy regarding

17
00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.669
screen-free learning can make mandatory online

18
00:00:55.669 --> 00:01:00.150
testing and required digital interventions challenging for parents to navigate.

19
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.459
Understanding these specific friction points is the first step

20
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:07.970
toward effectively supporting our Waldorf home study families.